        <h1><div class="anchor-anchor"><a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="anchor-link"><span class="fa fa-link"></span></a><span id="i2c-or-spi-374100" class="anchor-link-target"></span></div>I2C or SPI</h1>
<p><br>The nice thing about the 128x64 OLEDs is that they can be used with I2C (+ an optional reset line) or SPI. SPI is generally faster than I2C but uses more pins. It's also easier for some microcontrollers to use SPI. Anyways, you can use either one with this display<br><br></p>
<h2><div class="anchor-anchor"><a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="anchor-link"><span class="fa fa-link"></span></a><span id="using-with-i2c-374100" class="anchor-link-target"></span></div>Using with I2C</h2>
<p>The display can be used with any I2C microcontroller. Because the I2C interface is for 'writing' to the display only, you'll still have to buffer the entire 512 byte frame in the microcontroller RAM - you can't read data from the OLED (even though I2C is a bidirectional protocol).</p>
      











</div>

