        <p>Finally, connect the pins to your Arduino - </p>
<ul>
<li>
<strong>GND</strong> goes to ground</li>
<li>
<strong>Vin</strong> goes to 5V</li>
<li>
<strong>DATA</strong> to digital <strong>9</strong>
</li>
<li>
<strong>CLK</strong> to digital <strong>10</strong>
</li>
<li>
<strong>D/C</strong> to digital <strong>11</strong>
</li>
<li>
<strong>RST</strong> to digital <strong>13</strong>
</li>
<li>
<strong>CS</strong> to digital <strong>12</strong>
</li>
</ul>
<p><br><em>(<strong>Note</strong>: If using the display with other SPI devices, D/C, CLK and DAT may be shared, but CS must be unique for each device.)</em> <br><strong><br></strong>This matches the example code we have written. Once you get this working, you can try another set of pins. <br><br>Finally you can run the <strong><strong><strong>File→Sketchbook→Libraries→</strong><strong>Adafruit_SSD1306→SSD1306_128x64_spi</strong></strong></strong> example<strong><br></strong></p>
      
