<span>Finally, connect the pins to your Arduino -&nbsp;<strong>GND</strong>&nbsp;goes to ground,&nbsp;<strong>Vin</strong>&nbsp;goes to 5V,&nbsp;<strong>DATA</strong>&nbsp;to digital&nbsp;<strong>9</strong>,&nbsp;<strong>CLK</strong>&nbsp;to digital&nbsp;<strong>10</strong>,&nbsp;<strong>D/C</strong>&nbsp;to digital&nbsp;<strong>11</strong>,&nbsp;<strong>RST</strong>&nbsp;to digital&nbsp;<strong>13</strong>&nbsp;and finally&nbsp;<strong>CS</strong>&nbsp;to digital&nbsp;<strong>12.<br></strong></span><br><i>(<b>Note</b>: If using the display with other SPI devices, D/C, CLK and DAT may be shared, but CS must be unique for each device.)</i>&nbsp;&nbsp;<br><strong><br></strong>This matches the example code we have written. Once you get this working, you can try another set of pins.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br><br>Finally you can run the&nbsp;<strong><span><strong><b>File→Sketchbook→Libraries→</b><b>Adafruit_SSD1306→SSD1306_128x32_SPI&nbsp;</b></strong>example<br><strong><br><br></strong></span></strong>

    
