        <p><span><span> <span>Finally, connect the pins to your Arduino <br></span></span></span></p>
<ul>
<li><span><strong>GND</strong>&nbsp;goes to ground</span></li>
<li><span><strong>Vin</strong>&nbsp;goes to 5V</span></li>
<li><span><strong>SDA</strong> to I2C Data <strong>SDA</strong> pin (on the Uno, this is <strong>A4</strong> on the Mega it is&nbsp;<strong>20&nbsp;</strong>and on the Leonardo digital&nbsp;<strong>2</strong>)</span></li>
<li>
<span><strong>SCL</strong>&nbsp;</span> to I2C Clock <span> <strong>SCL</strong> pin</span> (on the Uno, this is <strong>A5</strong> on the Mega it is&nbsp;<strong>21&nbsp;</strong>and on the Leonardo digital&nbsp;<strong>3</strong>)</li>
<li><span><strong>RST</strong>&nbsp;to digital&nbsp;<strong>4</strong>&nbsp; (you can change this pin in the code, later)</span></li>
</ul>
<p><span><strong><br></strong>This matches the example code we have written. Once you get this working, you can change the RST pin. You cannot change the I2C pins, those are 'fixed' in hardware<br><strong><br></strong>Finally you can run the&nbsp;<strong><strong>File→Sketchbook→Libraries→</strong><strong>Adafruit_SSD1306→SSD1306_128x32_i2c&nbsp;</strong></strong>example. If you wired the RST pin to a GPIO pin, change <code>#define OLED_RESET &nbsp; &nbsp; -1</code>&nbsp; to the new pin number.<strong><br></strong></span></p>
      
