            <h2>
              <span id="python-setup">Python Setup</span>
            </h2>

            <section class="page-content">
                <div class="" role="region" aria-label="Page content">
      <div class="text-element">
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
    
    
        <p>You'll need to install the Adafruit_Blinka library that provides the CircuitPython support in Python. This may also require enabling I2C on your platform and verifying you are running Python 3. <a href="./circuitpython-on-raspberrypi-linux">Since each platform is a little different, and Linux changes often, please visit the CircuitPython on Linux guide to get your computer ready</a>!</p>
      
  
  
















</div>

      <div class="text-element">
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
        <h2><div class="anchor-anchor"><a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="anchor-link"><span class="fa fa-link"></span></a><span id="python-installation-of-ssd1306-library-3040824" class="anchor-link-target"></span></div>Python Installation of SSD1306 Library</h2>
<p>Once that's done, from your command line run the following command:</p>
      








</div>

      <div class="code-element" aria-label="Code element"
>
  <div class="download-code terminal">
    <div class="code-copy-container">
      <a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="code-copy-button"><i class="fa fa-clipboard"></i> Copy Text</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <pre class="code-text-only" style="display: none;">pip3 install adafruit-circuitpython-ssd1306</pre>
  <div class="code-preview-container"
>
    
    <pre class="prettyprint
                nohighlight terminal
                
                ">pip3 install adafruit-circuitpython-ssd1306</pre>
  </div>
  <div class="code-bottom-bar">
  </div>
</div>

      <div class="text-element">
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
  
        <p>If your default Python is version 3 you may need to run 'pip' instead. Just make sure you aren't trying to use CircuitPython on Python 2.x, it isn't supported!</p>
<p>If that complains about pip3 not being installed, then run this first to install it:</p>
      







</div>

      <div class="code-element" aria-label="Code element"
>
  <div class="download-code terminal">
    <div class="code-copy-container">
      <a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="code-copy-button"><i class="fa fa-clipboard"></i> Copy Text</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <pre class="code-text-only" style="display: none;">sudo apt-get install python3-pip</pre>
  <div class="code-preview-container"
>
    
    <pre class="prettyprint
                nohighlight terminal
                
                ">sudo apt-get install python3-pip</pre>
  </div>
  <div class="code-bottom-bar">
  </div>
</div>

      <div class="text-element">
  
  
  
  
  
  
        <h2><div class="anchor-anchor"><a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="anchor-link"><span class="fa fa-link"></span></a><span id="pillow-library-3041324" class="anchor-link-target"></span></div>Pillow Library</h2>
<p>We also need PIL, the Python Imaging Library, to allow using text with custom fonts. There are several system libraries that PIL relies on, so installing via a package manager is the easiest way to bring in everything:</p>
      





</div>

      <div class="code-element" aria-label="Code element"
>
  <div class="download-code terminal">
    <div class="code-copy-container">
      <a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="code-copy-button"><i class="fa fa-clipboard"></i> Copy Text</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <pre class="code-text-only" style="display: none;">sudo apt-get install python3-pil</pre>
  <div class="code-preview-container"
>
    
    <pre class="prettyprint
                nohighlight terminal
                
                ">sudo apt-get install python3-pil</pre>
  </div>
  <div class="code-bottom-bar">
  </div>
</div>

      <div class="text-element">
  
  
        <h2><div class="anchor-anchor"><a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="anchor-link"><span class="fa fa-link"></span></a><span id="numpy-library-3118478" class="anchor-link-target"></span></div>NumPy Library</h2>
<p>NumPy is needed for the <strong>circuitpython_typing</strong> library. This can be installed with the following command:</p>
      

</div>

      <div class="code-element" aria-label="Code element"
>
  <div class="download-code terminal">
    <div class="code-copy-container">
      <a href="./monochrome-oled-breakouts__21015941" class="code-copy-button"><i class="fa fa-clipboard"></i> Copy Text</a>
    </div>
  </div>
  <pre class="code-text-only" style="display: none;">sudo apt-get install python3-numpy</pre>
  <div class="code-preview-container"
>
    
    <pre class="prettyprint
                nohighlight terminal
                
                ">sudo apt-get install python3-numpy</pre>
  </div>
  <div class="code-bottom-bar">
  </div>
</div>

      <div class="text-element">
  
        <p>That's it. You should be ready to go.</p>
      
</div>

